# Target C — audited §162 explicit quantitative gap above the §146 Type-II endpoint

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only route module; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module is the collision-free normalization of the valid theorem developed on PR #426. It strengthens the §155 `+1/2000` Fejér endpoint separation to an explicit `+1/1250` gap by using near-Mantel concentration and a quantitative atom-versus-detour inequality.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\qquad
s:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},\qquad
C_{146}:={4\over9}(\pi+s)
=1.8195135379794445\ldots .
\]

Then every unbounded Type-II C-II survivor sequence satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{q-k\over D}
\ge C_{146}+{1\over1250}
=1.8203135379794445\ldots .
}
\tag{162.1}
\]

The theorem is independent of the carrier-decimal optimization. It does not close Type II or C-II.

---

## 162.1 Near the §146 endpoint, atomic train mass is almost minimal

Let

\[
\eta={1\over1250},\qquad t_D={q-k\over D}.
\]

Assume for contradiction that along a Type-II subsequence

\[
t_D\le C_{146}+\eta+o(1).
\tag{162.2}
\]

Pass further so all normalized currencies and empirical train-phase measures converge. Write

\[
\kappa=\lim{k\over D}\ge0,
\qquad x=\lim{T\over D}.
\]

The audited §146 stability inequalities give

\[
0\le\kappa\le9\eta
\tag{162.3}
\]

and

\[
\lambda:=\limsup {b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}
\le {9\over4}\eta.
\tag{162.4}
\]

The exact train and outer-walk ledgers are

\[
T=(q-k)+2f,\qquad Q=2(q-k)-b,\qquad f\le Q,
\]

and Type II gives `per(F)/D -> pi`. Hence

\[
x\le5t-2\pi.
\tag{162.5}
\]

Let `a` be the total atomic mass of the limiting empirical **probability** measure of train phases, and use absolute `D`-density masses

\[
A:=xa,\qquad N:=x(1-a).
\]

Audited §142 gives

\[
A\ge2s
\tag{162.6}
\]

and

\[
{q/T}\le2(1-a),
\]

so

\[
N\ge {t+\kappa\over2}.
\tag{162.7}
\]

Using (162.2), (162.5), and (162.7),

\[
A=x-N
\le{9\over2}t-2\pi-{\kappa\over2}
\le2s+{9\over2}\eta.
\tag{162.8}
\]

Likewise, from `A>=2s`,

\[
N=x-A
\le {C_{146}\over2}+5\eta.
\tag{162.9}
\]

No unconditional `1.9046256...` loaded-hull floor is used here.

---

## 162.2 Near-Mantel concentration leaves one dominant three-axis orbit

The exact projective `pi/3` relation decomposes the atomic support into disjoint three-point orbits. Let `m_j` be the absolute train-density mass in orbit `j`. Then

\[
\sum_jm_j=A.
\]

Weighted Mantel on the atomic crossing graph gives

\[
s^2=\Delta_2\le{1\over4}\sum_jm_j^2.
\tag{162.10}
\]

Let

\[
M=\max_jm_j,\qquad R=A-M.
\]

Since

\[
\sum_jm_j^2\le M\sum_jm_j=MA,
\]

we have

\[
M\ge{4s^2\over A},
\qquad
R\le A-{4s^2\over A}.
\]

The right-hand side is increasing for positive `A`. Put

\[
A_\eta:=2s+{9\over2}\eta,
\qquad
R_\eta:=A_\eta-{4s^2\over A_\eta}.
\tag{162.11}
\]

Then all train density outside one dominant three-axis orbit is at most

\[
B_\eta:={C_{146}\over2}+5\eta+R_\eta.
\tag{162.12}
\]

Every complete stopped train has at most two outer terminal occurrences. Therefore the outer-walk occurrence mass carried by the dominant orbit is at least

\[
L_\eta:=\pi-2B_\eta
=\pi-C_{146}-10\eta-2R_\eta.
\tag{162.13}
\]

After taking shrinking phase neighborhoods in the standard fixed-window-then-diagonal order, this becomes actual atomic mass of the limiting projective outer-edge direction measure on the three orbit directions. Consequently at least one outer-direction atom has mass

\[
\ell_\eta:={L_\eta\over3}.
\tag{162.14}
\]

Occurrence multiplicity is retained throughout; singleton trains and bridge occurrences are not dropped.

---

## 162.3 An outer-direction atom forces positive detour

Let `nu` be the normalized limiting outer-edge direction measure. Set

\[
K(\theta)=|\cos\theta|,\qquad F=K*\nu,\qquad g=F-2.
\]

Projection variation and Type-II width convergence give

\[
g\ge0.
\]

If `beta=nu(total)=lim b/D`, then

\[
E:=\int_0^\pi g(\theta)\,d\theta
=2(\beta-\pi)
=2\lim {b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}.
\]

Thus (162.4) gives

\[
E\le {9\over2}\eta.
\tag{162.15}
\]

On the projective period-`pi` circle,

\[
K''+K=2\delta_{\pi/2}.
\]

After translating `nu` by `pi/2` to a measure `mu`,

\[
g''+g=2(\mu-d\theta).
\tag{162.16}
\]

### Lemma 162.1 — atom-versus-detour

If `nu` has an atom of mass at least `ell`, with `0<ell<pi`, then

\[
\boxed{
E\ge {\ell^3\over2\pi^2+\ell^2}.
}
\tag{162.17}
\]

**Proof.** Put the corresponding atom of `mu` at zero, let `r=ell/2`, and use the period-`pi` test function

\[
\psi(\theta)=
\begin{cases}
\cos^2\!\left({\pi\theta\over2r}\right),&|\theta|\le r,\\
0,&r<|\theta|\le\pi/2.
\end{cases}
\]

It is `C^1`, piecewise `C^2`, nonnegative, satisfies

\[
\psi(0)=1,\qquad \int\psi=r,
\]

and

\[
\|\psi''+\psi\|_\infty
\le {2\pi^2\over\ell^2}+1.
\]

Pair (162.16) with `psi`. Positivity of `mu` gives

\[
2\ell
\le \int g(\psi''+\psi)+2\int\psi
\le\left({2\pi^2\over\ell^2}+1\right)E+\ell.
\]

Rearranging gives (162.17). `square`

Applying the lemma to (162.14) and using (162.15), a necessary condition for (162.2) is

\[
{9\over2}\eta
\ge
{\ell_\eta^3\over2\pi^2+\ell_\eta^2}.
\tag{162.18}
\]

---

## 162.4 Exact numerical separation at `eta=1/1250`

For

\[
\eta={1\over1250},
\]

recalculation gives

\[
R_\eta=0.0071932083502566\ldots,
\]

\[
L_\eta=1.2996926989098354\ldots,
\]

and

\[
\ell_\eta=0.4332308996366118\ldots .
\]

Therefore

\[
{\ell_\eta^3\over2\pi^2+\ell_\eta^2}
=0.0040805488365804\ldots,
\]

whereas

\[
{9\over2}\eta=0.0036.
\]

The contradiction margin is more than `4.8e-4`. The original proof branch also carries an exact-rational certificate based on Machin-series bounds for `pi` and rational squaring bounds for `sqrt(3)`; no rounded decimal is needed as a logical premise.

Hence (162.2) is impossible and (162.1) follows.

---

## 162.5 Audit disposition and interaction with nearby results

The proof was independently checked at the following fragile points.

- `A=xa` and `N=x(1-a)` use absolute train density; no probability/density factor is lost.
- Weighted Mantel is applied orbitwise to the exact projective `pi/3` atomic relation.
- The outer-terminal capacity is occurrence-counted. Each physical complete train contributes at most two outer terminal occurrences.
- Shrinking train-phase neighborhoods produce literal atoms of the limiting outer-direction measure; nonatomic train mass can only make the lower bound more conservative.
- The distributional identity has the correct factor two, and the compact `C^1` test function produces no endpoint delta terms.
- The `9/2` detour factor is correct because `int g` is twice the normalized excess `b-per(F)`.
- The constants in (162.11)–(162.18) were recomputed independently.
- The theorem does not use the rejected unconditional `1.9046256...` floor.

§155 remains the reusable all-regime Fejér tradeoff. The newer unnumbered atomic-cusp endpoint-charge theorem on `main` gives another continuous detour tradeoff but is much weaker numerically at the `1/1250` scale. The qualitative strict stopped-train phase gap is logically different: it separates the §142 train-phase currency without extracting an explicit constant.

**Final disposition:** PASS at audited-working proof-only Type-II scope. The explicit current lower bound from this theorem is

\[
\boxed{\liminf(q-k)/D\ge1.8203135379794445\ldots .}
\]

C-II and Level I remain open.